WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.890
We had the very unfortunate situation of having
a guest cancel on us last minute.

00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:09.790
And so we are going to pick up on a few phone
calls and see what's on people's, you can

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:20.110
call in at (617) 830-4750. Let's go to our
caller from the two six zero color from two

00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:23.120
six zero. What's your name? Where are you
calling from?

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:35.550
Hi, this is Joanna. Um, I have, um, family
member who is a libertarian and um, can you,

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:44.489
can you hear me okay. Okay. Um, so I like
really want Medicare for all and um, I talk

00:00:44.489 --> 00:00:50.430
about it a lot with them and um, they, their
argument is like, well why would you want

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:56.039
to give the government even more control?
Um, cause they're, they want small government.

00:00:56.039 --> 00:01:03.219
Um, and the only thing like I can come up
with is like, well, you know, if Congress

00:01:03.219 --> 00:01:07.610
had to have the same, you know, health care
that we did, they'd make sure that it was

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:14.110
like awesome, but I didn't know like what
else I could tell them to like get them onboard

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:15.110
with Medicare for all.

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:18.880
Yeah. I mean listen, you're probably not going
to be able to get them on board with Medicare

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:24.760
for all. I think that the extreme libertarian
position is one of the hardest ones to kind

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:29.530
of bring over to the side of wanting the government
to do things because they see the government

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:35.190
stepping out as good at it. They see the government
not being involved as virtuous regardless

00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:40.480
of the outcome. Even if what the free market
ends up putting together is inferior in some

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:44.500
way. They don't want the government involved
and believe the government can't do anything.

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:51.750
The reality is that um, the government is
not objective really bad at doing things.

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:57.980
It the, the problem is that we often have
a disagreement as to what different people

00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:02.960
believe our tax money should go to and we
end up having too much money going to certain

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:09.340
things like the military, the Pentagon, defense
weapons, military industrial complex, et cetera,

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:12.540
and not enough going towards things like healthcare.

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:19.150
Now as far as costs goes, other countries
where the government is more involved in healthcare,

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:24.890
spend less per person and have better outcomes.
So if their concern is that they don't think

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:29.719
the government would do a good job, there's
many counter examples that could be given.

00:02:29.719 --> 00:02:33.859
If their concern is that it's going to cost
too much, well then you can talk to them about

00:02:33.859 --> 00:02:37.989
the cost programs that are, are very reasonable
in terms of what something like this would

00:02:37.989 --> 00:02:41.590
cost. If they simply ideologically don't want
the government doing stuff that's going to

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:44.510
be a, you're going to hit a brick wall there.
I don't know what you could say.

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:45.510
Ooh,

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:53.709
gotcha. Yeah, that's helpful. Um, cause they,
you know, they obviously, you know, believe

00:02:53.709 --> 00:02:58.639
the government is corrupt. Um, and they agree
with me that healthcare should not be for

00:02:58.639 --> 00:03:04.110
profit. Um, and so I'm like well that's why
you don't want like private companies in charge

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:09.030
of healthcare because their whole goal is
to generate profit as much profit as possible.

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:13.700
Yeah. I mean, and most healthcare companies,
the industry in the U S is regulated in a

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:18.010
way that actually sort of limits the percentage
that health insurance companies can profit.

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:23.549
But just because we're talking about it, there
are other systems, like for example, one where

00:03:23.549 --> 00:03:29.890
the government, you still have non-government
insurance companies, they have to be nonprofit

00:03:29.890 --> 00:03:34.379
companies and they are heavily regulated by
the government. And the government still act

00:03:34.379 --> 00:03:37.909
sometimes as a payer, like there's all sorts
of different plans. You can look at what France

00:03:37.909 --> 00:03:41.419
does. Germany, Switzerland, there's lots of
different ideas. But again, if it's just an

00:03:41.419 --> 00:03:45.069
ideological barrier, it can be tough to talk
to libertarians.

00:03:45.069 --> 00:03:46.069
Hmm.

00:03:46.069 --> 00:03:48.709
All right. Thank you so much. I appreciate
it.

00:03:48.709 --> 00:03:50.970
All right, Joanna, I appreciate the call.
Thank you so much.

